### Title
Buffer overflow

### Severity
High

### Difficulty
Low

### Type
Access Controls

### Target
Pwnable Source/ShellCode_Required/socket_bof.c

## Description
Endstr holds 50 bytes, but sprintf writes a 25-byte literal prefix ("nom nom nom, you sent me ") plus up to 500 bytes of str — as much as ~525 bytes into a 50-byte buffer. Sprintf does no bounds checking, so anything over 24 bytes everwrites the stack, including the frame pointer and return address. The severity is high  mainly because the attacker has potentially full access to the str variable.

## Exploit Scenario
Attacker writes the message over 24 bytes, overwriting the return address and frame pointer, newly ppointing into malicious code in memory. He could potentially insert the code directly in the same message.

## Recommendations
use this snprintf(endstr, sizeof(endstr), "nom nom nom, you sent me %s", str); to maximally write the length of the string and not overwrite stack



